    story: `Al-Yasa' occupies one of the smallest textual footprints of any Prophet named directly in the Quran, appearing across only two brief verses, each grouping him alongside other honored figures rather than narrating any independent episode of his own life or mission. The first mention places him within a wider list gathering an entire chain of Prophets in a single sweeping passage: "And Isma'il and al-Yasa' and Yunus and Lut — and all [of them] We preferred over the worlds." (Quran 6:86) The second places him within a still smaller and more selectively honored circle: "And [mention] Isma'il and al-Yasa' and Dhul-Kifl, and all are among the outstanding." (Quran 38:48)

Despite this brevity, the classical tradition has consistently understood al-Yasa' to stand in a direct and specific relationship to Ilyas, generally described across the earlier tafsir literature as Ilyas's own successor, chosen and prepared by Ilyas himself to continue the same prophetic mission among the very people Ilyas had spent his own life calling to abandon their idol worship. This understanding of a direct succession between the two Prophets — one preparing and passing the mission on to the next, much as Musa's own mission would later be continued in certain respects by Yusha' ibn Nun (Joshua) after him — reflects a broader pattern the wider Islamic tradition recognizes across several of the shorter prophetic accounts: that a single mission to a single community was not always concluded within one Prophet's own lifetime, but was sometimes carried forward by a chosen and prepared successor once the founding Prophet's own life had ended.

The classical commentators, working from the accumulated but comparatively thin body of transmitted material available regarding al-Yasa' specifically, generally describe him continuing the same essential confrontation his predecessor had begun — calling the same community back from its worship of Ba'l toward the recognition of the One Creator responsible for every blessing they had falsely attributed to their idol — though the specific narrative episodes of his own individual ministry are recorded with considerably less detail across the tradition than the confrontations attributed to several of the other Prophets covered in this collection, and Ibn Kathir, in his own treatment of al-Yasa' within Qisas al-Anbiya, is candid in noting how comparatively little reliable material has survived regarding the specific unfolding of his own mission, in clear contrast to the fuller accounts preserved for figures such as Yusuf or Musa.

What the Quran itself establishes about al-Yasa' with complete certainty, however, requires no further narrative elaboration to carry its full weight: he is named directly among Allah's own chosen messengers, placed in the same select company as Isma'il, Idris, and Dhul-Kifl — each of them figures the Quran commends specifically and repeatedly for sustained patience and quiet righteousness rather than any single dramatic confrontation — and described, in a term the Quran applies sparingly and only to a genuinely select circle of its most honored figures, as being "among the outstanding" (mina al-akhyar). It is a brief mention, but a complete one: proof, alongside Idris and Dhul-Kifl before him in this same collection, that the Quran's honor roll of Prophets does not require an extended, dramatic narrative to establish a figure's genuine and lasting standing before Allah — sometimes a single sentence of direct divine commendation is entirely sufficient.`,
